<h2>The situation</h2>

<p>
  The SEO offering had been stable for years, and that was the problem. It was
  built around a monthly cadence of blog posts and local listings management
  &mdash; real work, genuinely useful when it was designed, and increasingly
  hard to defend as the thing a practice should buy in 2025.
</p>

<p>
  Two pressures made that gap visible at the same time. Search itself changed:
  AI answer surfaces, local pack volatility, and entity-driven visibility meant
  that publishing a post a month no longer moved the metrics a practice owner
  cares about. And the conversation changed: clients were being pitched more
  sophisticated-looking strategic offerings, and we had no artifact that made
  our own thinking visible.
</p>

<h2>What made it genuinely hard</h2>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Changing the service means changing the money.</strong> A new
    offering is not a deliverables list; it is a positioning claim and a price.
    Both had to be defensible against what the market was actually charging.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Specialists had to be able to deliver it.</strong> A strategy that
    only works when the person who designed it runs it is not a service. It
    needed to survive being handed to someone else on a Tuesday.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Clients had to understand it well enough to approve it.</strong>
    Practice owners and marketing managers do not approve crawl budget. They
    approve a plan they can see themselves in.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Practices are not at the same starting line.</strong> A single-office
    practice in a low-competition market and a multi-location group fighting
    three regional competitors need different recommendations out of the same
    framework.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Building the offering</h2>

<h3>Research before packaging</h3>
<p>
  I started by researching how comparable SEO offerings were structured,
  scoped, and priced &mdash; what was included, what was positioned as premium,
  and where the real differentiation sat. That research is what turned
  &ldquo;we should do more SEO&rdquo; into a specific recommendation on
  positioning and pricing structure rather than an opinion.
</p>

<h3>Enablement, not just a deck</h3>
<p>
  Alongside the positioning work I wrote the implementation guides and built
  the standardized client deliverables the service runs on. The point of
  standardizing is not uniformity for its own sake &mdash; it is that every
  client gets the same floor of quality regardless of who is assigned to the
  account, and specialists spend their time on judgment calls instead of
  rebuilding the same slides.
</p>

<h3>Selling it</h3>
<p>
  I supported sales and onboarding for the new service, helping present and
  close it with five clients to date. Sitting in those conversations is also
  the fastest feedback loop there is on whether a service design actually
  holds up: you find out immediately which slide raises a question nobody on
  the internal team had thought to ask.
</p>

<h2>The centerpiece: a 33-slide strategy check-in</h2>

<p>
  Within the modernized offering, the <strong>Web &amp; SEO Strategy
  Check-In</strong> deck is the artifact that makes the strategy real. It
  anchors a collaborative one-hour annual planning session bringing together
  the SEO specialist, their manager, the client's marketing manager, and
  practice stakeholders &mdash; to review year-over-year performance, align on
  next year's strategy, and secure approvals on recommended keyword
  opportunities.
</p>

<p>
  Structuring that conversation around diagnostics rather than a report is the
  whole design intent. The deck moves through:
</p>

<h3>Year-over-year performance and lead diagnostics</h3>
<p>
  Multi-year comparisons across search impressions, organic traffic, Google
  Business Profile visibility and engagement &mdash; calls, website clicks,
  driving directions &mdash; and website versus GBP lead trends. Each one is
  framed around seasonality, search behavior shifts, and conversion efficiency,
  so a decline arrives with an explanation attached rather than as a red arrow.
</p>

<h3>Local and competitive benchmarking</h3>
<p>
  Local Pack snapshots, ranking grid heatmaps covering average rank, best
  position, local pack presence, and share of voice for high-intent hearing
  care queries, plus competitor visibility and traffic comparisons. Geographic
  rank data earns its place in these meetings: a heatmap of where a practice
  does and does not show up is immediately legible to someone who has never
  opened a rank tracker.
</p>

<h3>Performance snapshot and strategy placemat</h3>
<p>
  A standardized scoring rubric across lead generation, office footprint, and
  local competition feeds a three-tier <strong>Strategy Placemat</strong>:
  <em>Building a Strong Foundation</em>, <em>Focused Optimization</em>, and
  <em>Expanded Performance Opportunities</em>. This is the piece that solves
  the &ldquo;not at the same starting line&rdquo; problem &mdash; the rubric
  decides which tier of recommendations a practice should be hearing, so advice
  scales with their situation instead of defaulting to the most expensive
  version of itself.
</p>

<h3>Modern search and conversion roadmap</h3>
<p>
  Guidance on 2026 local and AI search visibility signals, a hub-and-spoke
  content architecture linking location pages to evergreen resources,
  entity-based SEO with high-authority PR and citations, and conversion-moat
  tactics &mdash; interactive hearing quizzes, lead magnets, event landing
  pages, and targeted popups. The conversion half matters because organic
  visibility that does not convert is a vanity result for a practice whose
  outcome is a booked appointment.
</p>

<h3>Tiered content and keyword alignment</h3>
<p>
  Structured keyword recommendation slides, evergreen page options, quarterly
  custom content tiers, and Google Business Profile post themes tailored to
  practice goals. This section is where the meeting converts into next-year
  approvals, which is the actual purpose of the hour.
</p>

<h2>What I'd tell another strategist</h2>

<p>
  The instinct when modernizing a service is to start with the deliverables,
  because deliverables are concrete. That is backwards. Deliverables are
  downstream of a positioning decision, and if you skip the research that
  supports the positioning you end up with a better-looking version of the
  same offering at the same price.
</p>

<p>
  The second thing: build the client-facing artifact early and use it as the
  spec. A 33-slide deck forces every vague intention into a specific slide with
  a specific data source and a specific decision attached. If a strategic claim
  cannot survive being put in front of a practice owner with a number next to
  it, it was not ready to be part of the service.
</p>
